<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Geometry.Uv</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            UV unwrapping as three separable stages: where to cut, how a chart becomes flat, and
            where each island sits. Each one is a public entry point over a value a caller can hold
            and hand back, so "just repack these islands" is supported rather than a side effect of
            unwrapping. Margins are counted in texels, which is why the packer is told the atlas
            resolution.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>geometry;uv;unwrap;atlas;packing;texture;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
            ⚠ Optimised even in Debug, because this is a float inner loop and the unoptimised one is
            not a slower version of the same thing — it is the reason a local `Test` run measures
            this suite at 362 s and CI measures it at 98 s. Measured back to back on one machine:
            Debug 362 s, Debug with this property 96 s, Release 98 s, all 464 tests passing in each.
            The whole of the difference was the optimiser; nothing about it is algorithmic.

            ⚠ `Optimize` and `Configuration` are two decisions and only one of them is dangerous to
            move. `DEBUG` is still defined here, so `JobScheduler.SafetyChecksEnabled` and
            `LeakTracker.IsSupported` keep the values a Debug build gives them — which is what
            `CheckApi` records for those `public const bool`s and what several suites assert. This
            buys the Release speed without any suite crossing the configuration line.

            The cost is that stepping through this assembly is worse: locals fold away and the
            debugger's line table is approximate. That is the trade in a library whose public surface
            is three pure entry points over values, and it does not apply to callers — `Optimize` is
            per assembly.

            ⚠ And it does not generalise, which is why this is a named property here and not a
            setting in Directory.Build.props. #591 asked whether the rest of the tree pays the same
            3.7x. Measured the same way on 2026-09-05 — one machine, solo runs, every build forced
            with `NoIncremental` and the DLL size checked, so that the middle column is not the old
            binaries measured a second time:

                                       Debug   +Optimize   Release   tests
              Vixen.Raven.Tests         225 s      213 s     232 s    1921
              Vixen.Rendering.Tests     157 s      154 s        —     1428
              Vixen.Fuzz.Tests           50 s       41 s      35 s      42

            The two largest move by less than this machine's own spread — a parser and a frame-graph
            suite are bound by allocation, dictionaries and I/O, not by arithmetic the JIT can fold.
            Only the fuzz loop moves at all, by 1.24x, and its Release column shows more of that gap
            is `#if DEBUG` code than is the optimiser. So the lever is a float inner loop, not Debug.
        -->
        <Optimize Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">true</Optimize>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            docs/plan/42 § D2. Three references, and the direction matters more than the count:
            `Vixen.Geometry.Remeshing` references *this*, never the reverse, because the unwrapper
            has to run on meshes nobody remeshed and that is the entire point of the document.

            ⚠ `Vixen.Core.Threading` is not free — it carries `Vixen.Core` and
            `Vixen.Core.Diagnostics` with it, so the real closure is five assemblies rather than the
            three named here. `UvLayeringTests` states all five rather than letting the transitive
            pair arrive unremarked.

            ⚠ And `MeshData` is deliberately out of reach. It lives in `Vixen.Rendering`, one layer
            up, and the `EditMesh` bridge is in the editor. That is the correct side of the line to
            be on: a seam is two corners at one shared position with different coordinates, which
            `EditMesh` represents for nothing and a per-vertex structure represents by splitting.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Geometry\Vixen.Geometry.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Geometry.Uv.Tests" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **`Solving/` is a general sparse solver behind a UV unwrapper's front door, and doc 48
            § 4.6 is its second client.** `Normal → Height` is a grid Poisson: the same
            `SparseMatrix`, the same warm-started fixed-budget `ConjugateGradient`, assembled from
            texels instead of from triangles. Doc 42 § B1 built this precisely so the engine would
            own one of them, and writing a second conjugate gradient in the editor to avoid one line
            of csproj would be the outcome that document argued against.

            ⚠ **Every type under `Solving/` is `internal`, which is why this is an
            `InternalsVisibleTo` rather than a reference.** That is a wart and it is recorded as
            [#752](https://github.com/Rikarin/Vixen/issues/752): the honest fix is for the solver to
            live in an assembly of its own — nothing in `SparseMatrix`, `Preconditioner`,
            `ConjugateGradient` or `LeastSquaresSolver` mentions a chart, a triangle or a UV — and
            `Vixen.Geometry.Uv` would then be its first caller rather than its owner. Until then this
            line is what stops a second copy, and it is deliberately narrow: one named assembly, not
            a public surface `CheckApi` would then have to hold still.
        -->
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.TextureGraph" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
